\qs*
\q1 to whom {there are} no changes,
\q2 so they do not fear God.
\q1 \v 20 He stretches out his hands against his friends;
\q2 he defiles his covenant.
\q1 \v 21 The curds of his mouth were smooth,
\q2 but his heart {was} war.
\q1 His words were softer than oil,
\q2 but they {were} unsheathed {swords}.
\q1 \v 22 Cast your burden on Yahweh,
\q2 and he, he will sustain you.
\q2 Forever he will not allow shaking to the righteous.
\q1 \v 23 But you, God, will make them go down to the pit of the grave.
\q2 Men of blood and treachery will not halve their days.
\q1 But I, I will trust in you.
\c 56
\d For the chief musician; on “A Faraway Silent Dove.” Of David. A miktam. When the Philistines seized him in Gath.
\q1 \v 1 Be gracious to me, God, for man tramples me!
\q2 All of the day, fighting, he oppresses me.
\q1 \v 2 My enemies trample me all of the day,
\q2 for many are fighting against me arrogantly.
\q1 \v 3 The day I fear,
\q2 I, I will trust in you.
\q1 \v 4 In God—I praise his word—in God I trust.
\q2 I will not fear. What will flesh do to me?
\q1 \v 5 All of the day they vex my matters;
\q2 all of their thoughts {are} against me for harm.
\q1 \v 6 They gather together, they hide;
\q2 they, they watch my heels because they await my life.
\q1 \v 7 Upon iniquity {shall there be} an escape for them?
\q2 In nose, make the peoples go down, God.
\q1 \v 8 You, you record my wandering.
\q2 Put my tears in your bag.
\q2 {Are they} not in your record?
\q1 \v 9 Then my enemies will turn back on the day I call.
\q2 This I know, for God {is} for me.
\q1 \v 10 In God—I praise his word—
\q2 in Yahweh—I praise his word—
\q1 \v 11 in God I trust.
\q1 I will not fear. What will man do to me?
\q1 \v 12 Your vows {are} on me, God.
\q2 I will pay a thank-offering to you.
\q1 \v 13 For you deliver my life from death.
\q2 {Are} not my feet from stumbling,
\q2 that I may walk about to the face of God in the light of the living?
\c 57
\d For the chief musician; “May You Not Destroy.” Of David; a miktam. When he fled from the face of Saul, in the cave.
\q1 \v 1 Be gracious to me, God, be gracious to me,
\q2 for in you my soul takes refuge,
\q1 and in the shadow of your wings I take refuge
\q2 until the calamities have passed by.
\q1 \v 2 I call to God Most High,
\q2 to God, the one accomplishing for me.
\q1 \v 3 May he send from the heavens and save me.
\q2 May he reproach the one trampling me.
\qs Selah
\qs*
\q2 May God send his covenant faithfulness and his truth.
\q1 \v 4 My soul {is} in the midst of lions;
\q2 I must lie down {among} blazing ones, sons of man.
\q1 Their teeth {are} a spear and arrows,
\q2 and their tongue {is} a sharp sword.
\q1 \v 5 Be high above the heavens, God,
\q2 {may} your glory {be} above all of the earth.
\q1 \v 6 They prepare a net for my steps;
\q2 my soul bows down.
\q1 They dig a pit to my face;
\q2 they fall into the midst of it.
\qs Selah
\qs*
\q1 \v 7 My heart is established, God, my heart is established.
\q2 Let me sing and make music.
\q1 \v 8 Awake, my glory!
\q2 Awake, lyre and harp!
\q2 Let me awaken the dawn.
\q1 \v 9 I will thank you, Lord, among the peoples;
\q2 I will make music to you among the nations.
\q1 \v 10 For your covenant faithfulness {is} great to the heavens,
\q2 and your truth to the clouds.
\q1 \v 11 Be high above the heavens, God,
\q2 {may} your glory {be} above all of the earth.
\c 58
\d For the chief musician; “May You Not Destroy.” Of David; a miktam.
\q1 \v 1 Truly {in} silence do you speak righteousness?
\q2 Do you judge uprightly, sons of man?
\q1 \v 2 No, in the heart you do wickedness.
\q2 Your hands weigh out violence on the earth.
\q1 \v 3 The wicked turn aside from the womb;
\q2 they stray from the belly, speaking falsehood.
\q1 \v 4 The poison to them {is} like the likeness of the poison of a serpent.
\q2 {They are} like a deaf adder {that} shuts its ears,
\q1 \v 5 that does not listen to the voice of charmers,
\q2 one charming charms, being made wise.
\q1 \v 6 God, break their teeth in their mouth!
\q2 Break out the fangs of the young lions, Yahweh!
\q1 \v 7 May they be melted like waters {that} run off to them.
\q2 {When} he treads his arrows, {may they be} as if they are cut off.
\q1 \v 8 {May they be} like a snail {that} melts; it goes away.
\q2 {May they be like} the miscarriage of a woman; they do not see the sun.
\q1 \v 9 Before your pots perceive the thorn, like the living, like the burning,
\q2 he will whirlwind it away.
\q1 \v 10 The righteous will rejoice when he sees vengeance.
\q2 He will wash his feet in the blood of the wicked.
\q1 \v 11 Then man will say, “Truly, fruit {is} to the righteous.
\q2 Truly, there is a God judging the earth.”
\c 59
\d For the chief musician; “May You Not Destroy.” Of David; a miktam. When Saul sent and they watched the house to kill him.
\q1 \v 1 Deliver me from my enemies, my God!
\q2 Set me on high, away from the ones rising up against me.
\q1 \v 2 Deliver me from workers of iniquity,
